1.1.34.m:
Then having arisen, he set forth [saying]: 'This indeed I know: arising from these origins, from these couples, my Vedic student cannot die before [his full] lifespan.'

Brahman here beheld prosperity, firm standing, and an abode.
It heated [itself] with ascetic heat.
If it is maintained in an observance, it is firmly established in truth.
That Savitṛ, having created the brāhmaṇa by means of the Sāvitrī, wrapped that Sāvitrī around him.
'tat savitur vareṇyam' is the first quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī.
With the earth he joined the Ṛg verse;
with the Ṛg verse, Agni;
with Agni, prosperity;
with prosperity, woman;
with woman, a couple;
with a couple, offspring;
with offspring, ritual action;
with ritual action, ascetic heat;
with ascetic heat, truth;
with truth, Brahman;
with Brahman, the brāhmaṇa;
with the brāhmaṇa, the observance.
By the observance, indeed, the brāhmaṇa becomes sharpened.
He becomes not empty, he becomes unbroken; unbroken is his lineage, unbroken is his life, who knows thus and who, knowing thus, explains this first quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī in this way.

1.1.35.m:
'bhargo devasya dhīmahi' is the second quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī.
With the intermediate space he joined the Yajus formula;
with the Yajus formula, the wind;
with the wind, the cloud;
with the cloud, rain;
with rain, the herbs and forest trees;
with the herbs and forest trees, cattle;
with cattle, ritual action;
with ritual action, ascetic heat;
with ascetic heat, truth;
with truth, Brahman;
with Brahman, the brāhmaṇa;
with the brāhmaṇa, the observance.
By the observance, indeed, the brāhmaṇa becomes sharpened.
He becomes not empty, he becomes unbroken; unbroken is his lineage, unbroken is his life, who knows thus and who, knowing thus, explains this second quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī in this way.

1.1.36.m:
'dhiyo yo naḥ pracodayāt' is the third quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī.
With heaven he joined the Sāman chant;
with the Sāman chant, the sun;
with the sun, rays;
with rays, rain;
with rain, the herbs and forest trees;
with the herbs and forest trees, cattle;
with cattle, ritual action;
with ritual action, ascetic heat;
with ascetic heat, truth;
with truth, Brahman;
with Brahman, the brāhmaṇa;
with the brāhmaṇa, the observance.
By the observance, indeed, the brāhmaṇa becomes sharpened.
He becomes not empty, he becomes unbroken; unbroken is his lineage, unbroken is his life, who knows thus and who, knowing thus, explains this third quarter-verse of the Sāvitrī in this way.

1.1.38.m:
Indeed, those who do not know this think: 'We know this,' not knowing the actual reality.
This sacrifice is firmly established in the Vedas;
the Vedas are firmly established in speech;
speech is firmly established in mind;
mind is firmly established in breath;
breath is firmly established in food;
food is firmly established in the earth;
the earth is firmly established in the waters;
the waters are firmly established in light;
light is firmly established in wind;
wind is firmly established in space;
space is firmly established in Brahman;
Brahman is firmly established in the brāhmaṇa who knows Brahman.
He who knows thus is indeed one who knows Brahman.
He attains meritorious fame and fragrant scents;
with evil smitten away,
he enjoys endless prosperity, who knows thus and who, knowing thus, venerates this Sāvitrī, the mother of the Vedas, as attainment, as secret teaching. Thus the Brāhmaṇa.

1.2.22.m:
Therefore, a Brahmin should neither sing nor dance, lest he become an āglāgṛdha.

Therefore the oblation to Brahman is prior, and the one to Prajāpati is posterior;

the one to Brahman is indeed higher than the one to Prajāpati. Thus says the Brāhmaṇa.

The Atharvans and the Aṅgirases, the two eyes of Bhṛgu, then beheld Brahman.
They knew: 'We, the Bhṛgus and Aṅgirases, are indeed this entire universe.'
Those gods offered the oblation to Brahman in the Sāṃtapana fire.
This indeed is the oblation to Brahman, which is offered in the Sāṃtapana fire.
This indeed is the Sāṃtapana fire, namely the brāhmaṇa.
The gods partook of the refreshing strength of his refreshing drink, the Fathers, being well-disposed, partook of the svadhā-call, and through faith the brāhmaṇas partook of the heavenly world.
By this the seers press [soma]; at last women obtained it exclusively in themselves; outsiders press with both.
If the oblation to Brahman were not portioned out in the sacrifice, men would be born crooked from the oblation to Prajāpati.
'Hear, O yonder worlds!' For he is the father of the Āhavanīya, of the Gārhapatya, and of the Dakṣiṇa fire—he who offers the Agnihotra.
The gods rejoice in their beloved abode.
Of them, this Sāṃtapana fire is the foremost.
When his speech is satisfied, Agni is satisfied;
when breath is satisfied, Vāyu is satisfied;
when sight is satisfied, Āditya is satisfied;
when mind is satisfied, the Moon is satisfied;
when hearing is satisfied, the quarters and intermediate quarters are satisfied;
when unctuous fluids are satisfied, the waters are satisfied;
when the body-hairs are satisfied, the herbs and forest trees are satisfied;
when the body is satisfied, the earth is satisfied.
Thus this foremost Sāṃtapana fire, being satisfied, satisfies all who are satisfied—thus states the Brāhmaṇa. || 22

1.2.24.m:
Now Prajāpati, when about to sacrifice with Soma, said to the Vedas: "Whom among you shall I choose as Hotṛ, whom as Adhvaryu, whom as Udgātṛ, whom as brahman priest?" / They said: "Choose one who knows the Ṛgveda as Hotṛ, one who knows the Yajurveda as Adhvaryu, one who knows the Sāmaveda as Udgātṛ, and one who knows the Atharvans and Aṅgirases as brahman priest." / Thus his four-footed sacrifice firmly stands in the four worlds, in the four gods, in the four Vedas, in the four Hotṛ-offices. / He firmly stands with offspring and cattle who knows thus. / "Therefore choose one who knows the Ṛgveda as Hotṛ, for he knows the Hotṛ's office. / Agni is the Hotṛ. / The earth is the abode of the ṛc verses. / Agni is the deity, the gāyatrī is the metre, bhūr is the bright essence. / Therefore choose him alone as Hotṛ," [namely] for the winning of this world, / for the conquest of this world, / for the complete conquest of this world, / for the attainment of this world, / for the increase of this world, / for the prosperity of this world, / for the elevation of this world, / for the pervading of this world, / for the sufficiency of this world, / for the completion of this world. / "Now if he does not choose one who knows thus as Hotṛ, their sacrifice is emptied from the front. / Choose one who knows the Yajurveda as Adhvaryu, for he knows the Adhvaryu's office. / Vāyu is the Adhvaryu. / The intermediate space is the abode of the yajus formulas. / Vāyu is the deity, the triṣṭubh is the metre, bhuvaḥ is the bright essence.

Therefore choose him alone as Adhvaryu," [namely] "for this world..." and so on.

"Now if he does not choose one who knows thus as Adhvaryu, their sacrifice is emptied from behind.

Choose one who knows the Sāmaveda as Udgātṛ,

Heaven is the abode of the sāman chants.

Āditya is the deity, the jagatī is the metre, svaḥ is the bright essence.

Therefore choose him alone as Udgātṛ," [namely] "for this world..." and so on.

"Now if he does not choose one who knows thus as Udgātṛ, their sacrifice is emptied from the north.

Choose one who knows the Atharvans and Aṅgirases as brahman priest,

The Moon is the brahman priest.

The waters are the abode of the Bhṛgus and Aṅgirases.

The Moon is the deity, lightning and the uṣṇih and kakubh are the metres, Oṃ is the bright essence of the Atharvans, janat of the Aṅgirases.

Therefore choose him alone as brahman priest," for the winning of this world,

for the conquest of this world,

for the complete conquest of this world,

for the attainment of this world,

for the increase of this world,

for the prosperity of this world,

for the elevation of this world,

for the pervading of this world,

for the sufficiency of this world,

1.3.5.m:
The gods and the Asuras strove in battle.
There these three Hotrā offices fell into error.
Indra took away their ukthas and sāmans;
he gave them to the Hotṛ.
The Ājya [śastra] came to belong to the Hotṛ;
the Pra'uga to the Potṛ, the Vaiśvadeva came to belong to the Hotṛ;
the Niṣkevalya to the Neṣṭṛ;
the Marutvatīya came to belong to the Hotṛ;
the Agnimāruta to the Āgnīdhra.
Therefore this Agnimāruta is recited as if repeated.
Therefore these become as though reciting together: the Hotṛ, the Potṛ, and the Neṣṭṛ.
The Āgnīdhra was bewildered, [not knowing what] he should wear.
That was, as it were, the sphere of the brahman priest.
He took away half of them, and left behind the first honorarium, the first word, and this sacrificial fee. Thus [says] the Brāhmaṇa.

1.3.17.m:
Now further, the Kārus, indeed, were ṛṣis of small property.
They saw this one-part Agniṣṭoma.
They took it;
they sacrificed with it;
they went to heaven.
If someone should desire: 'May I go to heaven', he should sacrifice with this one-part Agniṣṭoma. Such is the Brāhmaṇa. || 17
